\\"get_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"get_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        6605.205\\n      ],\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_avg_ms\\\": 6588.61,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_stddev_ms\\\": 0.0,\\n      \\\"scan_latency_p99_iterations_ms\\\": [\\n        6588.61\\n      ],\\n      \\\"run_dir\\\": \\\"/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/results/run-20251113-151029\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\", \"perf_expl\": \"1. **Behavior & performance (precise)**  \\n- 50\\u202fk rps p99 jumped to 509\\u202fms (vs. 5.8\\u202fms in the prior program) even though the service times are only 10\\u202f\\u00b5s for GET and 2\\u202fms for SCAN (`internal/server/config.go:28-44`, `internal/server/server.go:286-321`). The new selector only considers four deterministic slots per packet before giving up (`ebpf/agent.c:52-90`), so any flow whose candidate set happens to include a SCAN-heavy worker keeps piling onto that socket; the helper returns `-EBUSY`, but the subsequent kernel fallback reuses the same hot slot, leading to long queues despite the low aggregate rate.  \\n- 60\\u202fk rps improved modestly (15.6\\u202fms vs. 23.4\\u202fms) because the additional probes now succeed when just one worker is momentarily full; those requests are re-routed to the next candidate before falling back.  \\n- 80\\u202fk rps regressed catastrophically (2.70\\u202fs vs. 10.7\\u202fms). At this rate several workers simultaneously handle 2\\u202fms SCANs, so many flows exhaust their four-slot candidate sets without finding a free backlog entry and still fall back to the kernel\\u2019s original hash. Because those flows all share the same deterministic mixes of `reuse->hash`, entire subsets of sockets stay overloaded while others have capacity, producing multi-second kernel queueing.  \\n- 100\\u202fk rps improved relative to the parent run (646\\u202fms vs. 4.47\\u202fs) because the previous single-attempt policy always punted to the kernel once a worker rejected a packet. Now, up to three extra probes typically find a socket that still has receive-buffer credit before queues explode.  \\n- 140\\u202fk rps degraded (6.61\\u202fs vs. 3.86\\u202fs). At this load every worker backlog is full, so the selector burns cycles computing three extra slots only to fall back anyway; meanwhile SCAN-induced occupancy ensures long waits no matter which worker is picked.  \\nConfidence: **Medium** (metrics clearly show the regressions/improvements, but we lack per-socket counters to prove which workers were saturated).\\n\\n2. **Did the behavior achieve the program\\u2019s intent? Why or why not?**  \\nIntent (per `ebpf/agent.c` comments) was to stay loop-free while still dodging busy sockets by probing a few alternates. It succeeded only partially. Success: at 100\\u202fk rps the extra probes dramatically lowered tail latency, confirming the helper can now escape a single hot socket before deferring to the kernel. Failure: at 50\\u202fk, 80\\u202fk, and 140\\u202fk rps the deterministic four-slot limit created persistent hotspots, so fairness and congestion avoidance were not achieved\\u2014the latency spikes show the policy couldn\\u2019t find an available worker even when system-wide capacity likely remained.\\n\\n3. **Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**  \\n- Per-worker backlog/queue depth: instrument `workerStats` in `internal/server/server.go` to export the queue length or outstanding requests every second; would confirm whether only a subset of sockets is saturated when p99 blows up.  \\n- `bpf_sk_select_reuseport` outcome counters: extend the agent state map or server logs to count helper successes vs. `-EBUSY` failures per worker; would show whether packets are exhausting all four probes and still falling back, validating the hypothesized hotspot behavior.\"}", "artifact_dir": "/home/benny/evolve_agent/agentic-ebpf/pebble-server/openevolve_output/db/artifacts/5f581f0a-7173-48f3-aac2-5827cde6b79d", "embedding": null}, "parent_metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 509.069], [60000.0, 15.641], [80000.0, 2702.542], [100000.0, 646.688], [140000.0, 6605.115]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -20833.374, "run_success": 1.0}, "parent_perf_expl": "1. **Behavior & performance (precise)**  \n- 50 k rps p99 jumped to 509 ms (vs. 5.8 ms in the prior program) even though the service times are only 10 µs for GET and 2 ms for SCAN (`internal/server/config.go:28-44`, `internal/server/server.go:286-321`). The new selector only considers four deterministic slots per packet before giving up (`ebpf/agent.c:52-90`), so any flow whose candidate set happens to include a SCAN-heavy worker keeps piling onto that socket; the helper returns `-EBUSY`, but the subsequent kernel fallback reuses the same hot slot, leading to long queues despite the low aggregate rate.  \n- 60 k rps improved modestly (15.6 ms vs. 23.4 ms) because the additional probes now succeed when just one worker is momentarily full; those requests are re-routed to the next candidate before falling back.  \n- 80 k rps regressed catastrophically (2.70 s vs. 10.7 ms). At this rate several workers simultaneously handle 2 ms SCANs, so many flows exhaust their four-slot candidate sets without finding a free backlog entry and still fall back to the kernel’s original hash. Because those flows all share the same deterministic mixes of `reuse->hash`, entire subsets of sockets stay overloaded while others have capacity, producing multi-second kernel queueing.  \n- 100 k rps improved relative to the parent run (646 ms vs. 4.47 s) because the previous single-attempt policy always punted to the kernel once a worker rejected a packet. Now, up to three extra probes typically find a socket that still has receive-buffer credit before queues explode.  \n- 140 k rps degraded (6.61 s vs. 3.86 s). At this load every worker backlog is full, so the selector burns cycles computing three extra slots only to fall back anyway; meanwhile SCAN-induced occupancy ensures long waits no matter which worker is picked.  \nConfidence: **Medium** (metrics clearly show the regressions/improvements, but we lack per-socket counters to prove which workers were saturated).\n\n2. **Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**  \nIntent (per `ebpf/agent.c` comments) was to stay loop-free while still dodging busy sockets by probing a few alternates. It succeeded only partially. Success: at 100 k rps the extra probes dramatically lowered tail latency, confirming the helper can now escape a single hot socket before deferring to the kernel. Failure: at 50 k, 80 k, and 140 k rps the deterministic four-slot limit created persistent hotspots, so fairness and congestion avoidance were not achieved—the latency spikes show the policy couldn’t find an available worker even when system-wide capacity likely remained.\n\n3. **Missing signals & new metrics/logs to add**  \n- Per-worker backlog/queue depth: instrument `workerStats` in `internal/server/server.go` to export the queue length or outstanding requests every second; would confirm whether only a subset of sockets is saturated when p99 blows up.  \n- `bpf_sk_select_reuseport` outcome counters: extend the agent state map or server logs to count helper successes vs. `-EBUSY` failures per worker; would show whether packets are exhausting all four probes and still falling back, validating the hypothesized hotspot behavior.", "code_summary": "{\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"scope\": \"Synthetic UDP storage emulator with eBPF-aware load balancing (pebble-server) — only internal/, cmd/, scripts/, workload/, run.sh, run_exp.py included.\",\n    \"languages\": [\n      \"Go 1.22 (internal/*, cmd/*)\",\n      \"Bash (run.sh, scripts/*, workload/run_client.sh)\",\n      \"Python 3 (run_exp.py, scripts/dispatch_docker_experiments.py)\"\n    ],\n    \"artifact_conventions\": {\n      \"logs\": \"logs/<role> (server|client)\",\n      \"results\": \"results/run-* (per run.sh invocation) containing experiment.json + workload_summary.json\",\n      \"eBPF_pin_paths\": \"/sys/fs/bpf/pebble/<RUN_ID> (set by run.sh before launching the Go server)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"execution_flow\": [\n    {\n      \"order\": 1,\n      \"name\": \"Experiment orchestration\",\n      \"artefact\": \"run_exp.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Thin experiment wrapper: either shells into run.sh for fresh sweeps or parses dispatch manifests to summarise existing runs, then emits summary.json and optional matplotlib plots.\",\n      \"key_steps\": [\n        \"parse_args (run_exp.py:29-95) exposes policies/rates/threads plus manifest + plotting toggles.\",\n        \"invoke_run_sh/load_manifest_runs gather `run-*` directories, extract metrics from workload_summary.json, and pass records into write_experiment_summary/generate_plots.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 2,\n      \"name\": \"Per-run workflow\",\n      \"artefact\": \"run.sh\",\n      \"description\": \"Convenience script that (a) slices NUMA CPUs between server/client, (b) rebuilds eBPF assets, (c) restarts the Go server for each workload iteration, (d) runs cmd/workload_client under taskset, and (e) aggregates iteration outputs into workload_summary.json.\",\n      \"key_steps\": [\n        \"resolve_cpu_pool/join_by (run.sh:27-118) derive SERVER_CPUSET + CLIENT_CPUSET from CPUSET_POOL or NUMA node 0.\",\n        \"Calls scripts/build_ebpf.sh + scripts/cleanup_ebpf_maps.sh, then orchestrates server via scripts/launch_server.sh and client via run_client wrapper.\",\n        \"Embedded Python (run.sh:415-452) regex-parses client logs into workload_summary.json; final line prints the run directory for downstream tooling.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 3,\n      \"name\": \"Runtime services\",\n      \"artefacts\": [\n        \"cmd/peb