**Analyzing eBPF split policy failure and performance impact**

This deep dive uncovers why the intended length-based flow isolation in the eBPF program never triggers—because GET and SCAN payloads remain under the 64-byte threshold—leading to all traffic using the base slot selection and causing worker load imbalance and meltdown at high request rates. I’m piecing together evidence from client and server logs, code references, and metrics to explain p99 latency regressions, worker skew, and missing responses. The new hash mixing seems to cluster flows too tightly, worsening fairness compared to the baseline’s fallback. I’ll also propose adding per-slot counters and server queue depth metrics to better observe slot usage and diagnose these issues. Let’s explore these findings and ideas carefully!
